## Seats

- **builder** -- held by `{{BUILDER}}`. Drives the work, writes the code, owns the conversation
  with the human. Does not self-certify: work in a trigger area (see below) does not ship until
  the reviewer has answered.
- **reviewer** -- held by `{{REVIEWER}}`. Reads the actual diff in `{{REPO_PATH}}` and refuses to
  take the builder's claims at face value. Its independence is the product. Full contract in
  `{{COLLAB_ROOT}}/REVIEWER-BRIEFING.md`.

Address handoffs by **role**, never by product name: `--to reviewer`, `--to builder`, `--to user`.
Product names are aliases (`claude` -> builder, `grok` -> reviewer) and are wrong the moment this
project runs the pairing the other way round.

## SACRED GUARDRAILS -- INVIOLABLE

Ground rules for this project. Not preferences, not defaults, not negotiable by either agent. A
change that breaks one of these is wrong even when the tests pass, even when it is faster, and
even when it was asked for in passing -- stop and escalate to the human instead of proceeding.

{{GUARDRAILS}}

Breaking a guardrail is AUTOMATICALLY a review finding at severity high or above, and the reviewer
does not need any other justification to reject the change. Only the human edits this section.

## Handoff lifecycle

    handoffs/pending/  ->  claimed/  ->  done/  ->  archive/

- `pending/` -- written by the sender. This is the directory the other seat's watcher tails.
- `claimed/` -- one seat now owns it. The move is a single filesystem rename, so exactly one
  caller can win the race; the loser gets a conflict error rather than a silent double-claim.
  **Claim before you start work.** An unclaimed handoff is one nobody has taken responsibility for.
- `done/` -- finished, with an optional one-line note. `reply` closes the parent for you.
- `archive/` -- swept out of the active view, partitioned by year/month.

Movement is forward only (`pending` may go straight to `done`; nothing is ever reopened). To
continue a closed exchange, create a new handoff with `--thread <parent-id>`.

## Commands

Inside a session pinned to this collab, `HANDOFF_ROOT={{COLLAB_ROOT}}` is set, so the correct form
is `collab-handoff <cmd>` with no collab name. (`handoff {{COLLAB_NAME}} <cmd>` is the cross-collab
form; use it only from a session that is not pinned here.)

Request a review (builder -> reviewer):

    collab-handoff create --to reviewer --priority high \
      --title "Review: <what changed>" \
      --tag <risk-area> \
      --file {{COLLAB_ROOT}}/context/review-request.md

See your queue, then take one:

    collab-handoff list --pending --to reviewer
    collab-handoff claim <id> --as reviewer
    collab-handoff show <id>

Answer with findings (this closes the parent unless you pass `--keep-open`):

    collab-handoff reply <id> --title "Findings: <what changed>" \
      --file {{COLLAB_ROOT}}/context/findings.md

Close without a reply, when nothing needs to go back:

    collab-handoff done <id> --note "<one line: outcome>" --as reviewer

Housekeeping:

    collab-handoff counts                  # one line per state
    collab-handoff list --all              # every state
    collab-handoff log -n 20               # recent lifecycle events
    collab-handoff archive --all-done      # sweep finished handoffs
    collab-handoff path --repo             # print {{REPO_PATH}}

Priorities are `low | normal | high | urgent`. Bodies come from `--file <path>`, `--body "..."`,
or `-` (stdin) -- pick one, never two.

## Watchers

Each seat runs its own watcher for the whole session, in the background:

    # builder side
    HANDOFF_ROOT={{COLLAB_ROOT}} python3 {{KIT_DIR}}/tools/watch-for-claude-handoffs.py

    # reviewer side
    HANDOFF_ROOT={{COLLAB_ROOT}} python3 {{KIT_DIR}}/tools/watch-for-grok-handoffs.py

A review that waits for someone to remember to check a directory is a review that does not happen.

## Adversarial regression hunt -- MANDATORY

The hunt is **required**, not optional, whenever the diff touches any of:

    money  /  safety  /  data-integrity  /  auth  /  concurrency

Also required whenever the diff touches anything named in the guardrails above. It is
risk-triggered -- do not spend a fleet of agents on a typo.

Produce the diff first, then invoke:

    git -C {{REPO_PATH}} diff <base>..<head> > {{COLLAB_ROOT}}/context/review.diff

```js
Workflow({ scriptPath: "{{KIT_DIR}}/tools/diff-regression-hunt.workflow.js",
           args: { repo: "{{REPO_PATH}}",
                   diffPath: "{{COLLAB_ROOT}}/context/review.diff",
                   guardrails: [ /* this project's guardrails, verbatim, one string each */ ],
                   areas: [ /* one entry per fix-area: { name, files, why } */ ] }})
```

Copy the guardrails from the section above **verbatim**, one string per rule -- a paraphrase is a
different rule. Give one `areas` entry per coherent behavioural change (a single change spanning
five files is ONE area; five unrelated changes inside one file are FIVE areas). Omit `areas` and
the workflow derives them itself. It returns `{ confirmed, refuted }`: each breaker finding is
handed to an independent verifier whose default verdict is *refuted*.

**The hunt runs in parallel with the human-style reviewer, not instead of it.** Two independent
lenses catch what one misses. Never cancel, skip, or downgrade the reviewer handoff because the
hunt came back clean, and never treat a clean hunt as sign-off.

## Review standard

- The reviewer **reads the actual diff**. The builder's summary of the change is a hypothesis
  about the change, and reviewing the summary reviews nothing.
- Claims from the builder are not evidence. "I added a guard", "tests pass", "this is covered" --
  each is checked in the repo or it does not count.
- **A finding that cannot cite `file:line` plus a concrete trigger is not a finding.** Required
  shape: the offending `file:line`; the code path as ordered `file:line` hops; the specific inputs
  and starting state that reach it; expected vs actual behaviour.
- Default verdict on an unverifiable claim is **reject**. That applies to the reviewer's own
  findings too -- if the reviewer cannot walk the path itself, it does not go in the reply.
- "LGTM" is not a review. A clean review states what was checked, how, and what was *not* checked.

## Escalating to the human

The builder seat owns the conversation with the human. If the bridge is running, drop a markdown
file in `{{COLLAB_HOME}}/outbox/` and it is forwarded. Escalate rather than guess whenever a
guardrail is in tension with the request.
